Day 31–40: Introduction to NoSQL with MongoDB  

Topic:  
Introduction to NoSQL databases with a focus on MongoDB, covering its architecture, data modeling, and CRUD operations.  

Learning Objectives:  
• Understand the differences between SQL and NoSQL databases.  
• Learn MongoDB’s document-based data structure and querying methods.  
• Gain hands-on experience in performing CRUD operations in MongoDB.  

Activities / Deliverables:  
• Install MongoDB and set up a local environment.  
• Create and manage collections in MongoDB.  
• Perform CRUD operations using MongoDB Shell or MongoDB Compass.  
• Design a simple document-based schema for a sample application.  

Important Concepts:  
• Document-oriented database  
• Collections and documents in MongoDB  
• JSON-like BSON format  

SQL Syntax / Commands:  
• N/A (MongoDB uses its own query language, e.g., `db.collection.find()`, `db.collection.insertOne()`)  

Real-world Use Cases:  
• Storing unstructured or semi-structured data like user profiles or product catalogs.  
• Scalable data storage for applications with high read/write operations.  

Tools / Tech:  
• MongoDB  
• MongoDB Compass  
• MongoDB Shell  

Summary:  
Explored the fundamentals of NoSQL databases and MongoDB, practiced basic operations, and understood its use cases in modern applications.  

